{"summary":"Reject hexadecimal prefixes with no digits","explanation":"The four failing PrefixOnly tests cover signed and unsigned 32-bit and 64-bit parsers. For `0x` with bases 0 and 16, and for signed `-0x` with those bases, the assertions compare `line.status` (expected false) with `status` (observed true). The shared sign/base parser strips the prefix and returns an empty view; the positive and negative digit parsers then accept that empty view as zero. Reject an empty payload immediately after consuming a hexadecimal prefix. This preserves lone `0` handling for inferred base and leaves ordinary digit parsing, overflow checks, and unsigned negative-sign rejection intact.","edits":[{"path":"absl/strings/numbers.cc","old":"    if (end - start >= 2 && start[0] == '0' &&
        (start[1] == 'x' || start[1] == 'X')) {
      base = 16;
      start += 2;
    } else if (end - start >= 1 && start[0] == '0') {","new":"    if (end - start >= 2 && start[0] == '0' &&
        (start[1] == 'x' || start[1] == 'X')) {
      base = 16;
      start += 2;
      if (start >= end) {
        return false;
      }
    } else if (end - start >= 1 && start[0] == '0') {"},{"path":"absl/strings/numbers.cc","old":"    if (end - start >= 2 && start[0] == '0' &&
        (start[1] == 'x' || start[1] == 'X')) {
      start += 2;
    }
  } else if (base >= 2 && base <= 36) {","new":"    if (end - start >= 2 && start[0] == '0' &&
        (start[1] == 'x' || start[1] == 'X')) {
      start += 2;
      if (start >= end) {
        return false;
      }
    }
  } else if (base >= 2 && base <= 36) {"}]}
